.TH MULL 1 "November 9, 2026" "MuLL" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mull \- interpret MuLL or compile it through MuLLIER and Mu3AC
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mull
.RI [ options ] " file " [ "-- arguments..." ]
.br
.B mull
.RI [ options ] " -e source " [ "-- arguments..." ]
.br
.B mull --repl
.SH DESCRIPTION
MuLL is a dynamically typed, lexically scoped language hosted by MuDSL.
The default host is the C++20 implementation in MuDSL.hpp; the D implementation
in MuDSL.d is selected explicitly. There is no silent fallback between hosts.
The default backend interprets the analyzed MuLLIER tree.
.PP
The native backend lowers that same tree to Mu3AC, a register-based
three-address
representation with explicit branches, scopes, closures, and call parameters.
It emits GNU assembler syntax for Linux x86-64 using the System V AMD64 ABI,
then invokes an assembler and the selected host's linker driver.
Generated code uses the selected host's value/runtime services; it does not
reparse and interpret the original entry-point source.
Imported MuLL packages continue to use the host's package evaluator.
.PP
With no file, standard input is read, or an interactive interpreter is started
when standard input is a terminal. A file named minus also denotes standard
input. Script arguments must follow a separate double dash, after the source
file or evaluation option. They are available as the immutable global
.B Args
list; the executable name is not included. The host service record is
.BR G .
.SH HOST AND BACKEND OPTIONS
.TP
.BR --engine=cpp | d " (also --host)"
Select MuDSL.hpp or MuDSL.d. C++ is the default.
The D worker must have been built with a D compiler.
.TP
.BR --backend=interpreter | x86-64
Choose tree evaluation or native compilation and execution.
Without an emission option the native executable is temporary and is run
immediately. Explicit assembly/object/executable emission selects the native
backend unless a conflicting interpreter backend was requested.
.TP
.B --list-engines
Show which host workers and native runtime archives are installed.
.TP
.B --target=x86_64-linux-gnu
Select the currently supported native target. Other targets are rejected.
.SH COMPILATION STAGES
.TP
.BI --emit= stage
Write an artifact and stop. Stages are
.BR ast , " mullier", " mu3ac", " asm", " obj", " exe".
The AST is the parsed tree in MuLLIER's S-expression interchange format.
MuLLIER output has undergone semantic analysis and optional optimization.
Mu3AC output is versioned, flat, per-function three-address code.
Assembly is GNU/ELF text; objects are ELF relocatables; executable output
links the corresponding C++ or D native runtime.
.TP
.BI --stop-after= stage
Equivalent names are
.BR parse , " mullier", " mu3ac", " assembly", " object", " link".
.TP
.B --check
Parse and analyze without evaluating, importing packages, or emitting output.
It is not a proof that late-bound names, dynamic types, or imports will succeed.
.TP
.B -S
Equivalent to --emit=asm.
.TP
.B -c
Equivalent to --emit=obj.
.TP
.BI -o " file"
Write an artifact to file. Text artifacts default to standard output;
objects default to a.o and executables to a.out.
Binary artifacts cannot be written to standard output.
The source file cannot also be the output file.
.TP
.B --run
Run the executable after linking. This cannot be combined with a stopping
stage earlier than executable emission.
.TP
.B -O0
Disable MuLLIER and Mu3AC transformations. Semantic analysis remains enabled.
.TP
.B -O1
Enable bounded optimization, the default. Default Mu3AC rules simplify only
local, already-evaluated scalar expressions; they do not reassociate floating
point arithmetic, reorder effects, or discard runtime checks without a fact.
.SH SOURCE AND EXECUTION
.TP
.BI "-e, --eval " source
Use the argument as source. Cannot be combined with a source file.
.TP
.B --repl
Persistent interpreter, one complete MuLL program per input line.
Bindings survive subsequent lines. Type :quit to exit.
REPL mode is not available for compile-only or native modes.
.TP
.B -p, --print-result
Print the final value followed by a newline. Without this option only explicit
program output is printed. A numeric return value is not a process exit code.
.SH PACKAGES AND THIRD-PARTY LIBRARIES
.TP
.BI "-I, --package-path " directory
Add a package discovery root, repeatable. Explicit roots precede MULL_PATH
and the installed standard-library root. A package named example is found at
directory/example/Package.mull. The current directory is not implicitly added.
.TP
.B --no-default-paths
Omit MULL_PATH and the installed standard-library root; keep explicit -I roots.
.TP
.B --no-stdlib
Do not register native standard-library records. This does not remove
discovery roots or the core G services.
.TP
.BI --load " package"
Preload a package at execution time, repeatable. Compile-only stages never
evaluate these packages. A preload failure terminates execution.
.TP
.BI "-L, --library-path " directory
Add a native linker search directory.
.TP
.BI "-l, --library " name
Link a native archive or shared library by name.
Package discovery (-I) and native linking (-L/-l) are different mechanisms.
Native linker options require the native backend.
.TP
.BI --link-object " file"
Link an additional object or archive, repeatable.
.TP
.BI --extern= name=symbol:arity
Expose a native C-ABI function as a MuLL global name.
The symbol must have exactly zero through eight double arguments and return
one double. Argument counts, numeric MuLL inputs, and finite results
are checked.
For example, --extern=Square=custom_square:1 binds double custom_square(double).
C++ functions must use extern "C". Arbitrary C structs, variadic functions,
pointer signatures, C++/D objects, and exceptions across this foreign ABI are
not supported. A false signature can cause undefined behavior: this is a
trusted FFI contract, not automatic header introspection or a sandbox.
Names G and Args are reserved.
.TP
.BI --rpath " directory"
Add a runtime shared-library search path to a linked executable.
.TP
.BI --link-arg " argument"
Pass one argument directly to the host compiler during linking, repeatable.
Use --link-arg=ARG when ARG starts with a minus.
No shell splitting is performed.
.SH COMPILER AND RESOURCE OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --mullier-rules " directory"
Replace MuLLIER rules from sorted .a and .t files. Both kinds are required.
Reloading is transactional. Native package evaluation also uses this directory;
it must remain available if the executable imports packages.
.TP
.BI --mu3ac-rules " file"
Replace local Mu3AC rewrite equalities with a trusted MuDSL rule file, at most
one MiB. Only supported scalar replacement forms are accepted by the optimizer.
User equalities are compiler extensions and need not preserve
language semantics.
.TP
.BI --cc " executable"
Assembler driver, default CC or cc.
.TP
.BI --cxx " executable"
C++ linker driver, default CXX or the configured compiler.
.TP
.BI --dc " executable"
D linker driver, default DC or the configured compiler.
Native D archives are built with LDC or DMD; a GDC-only installation provides
the D interpreter/compiler worker but not the native D runtime archive.
.TP
.BI --max-steps= N
Execution work budget; default 1000000. Native entry code and interpreted
package/callback work share a budget. Optimized and unoptimized work counts
need not be identical.
.TP
.BI --max-call-depth= N
Maximum nested script calls; default 128.
.TP
.BI --max-frames= N
Cumulative lexical-environment allocation budget; default 100000.
.TP
.BI --max-source-bytes= N
Maximum source/package input size; default 4194304 bytes.
.TP
.BI --max-values= N
Native handle-arena budget; default 2000000. Handles and their values remain
alive for the native execution context. This option does not limit the tree
interpreter's host allocations.
.PP
All numeric limits must be positive. Native compilation also bounds functions
(4096), registers per function (4096), instructions (262144), and arguments,
parameters, or entries in a collection literal (256).
Large runtime collections can still be built by library functions.
Limits are not timeouts and do not bound arbitrary foreign/native code or
the operating system's stack. Neither backend is a security sandbox.
.TP
.B -v, --verbose
Print argument vectors for invoked tools on standard error.
.TP
.B --help
Print usage and all options.
.TP
.B --version
Print the command version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B MULL_PATH
Colon-separated package roots after explicit -I roots and before the installed
standard library. Empty entries are ignored, not interpreted as
the current directory.
.TP
.BR CC , " CXX", " DC"
Default assembler and host linker executables. Each value names one executable,
not a shell command with embedded flags.
.TP
.B MULL_CONFIG
Override the worker/runtime installation descriptor, primarily for development.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
mull -p -e 'return 6*7;'
mull --engine=d -p app.mull -- input.txt
mull --stop-after=mullier app.mull > app.mullier
mull --emit=mu3ac -O0 app.mull > app.mu3ac
mull --emit=exe -o app -p app.mull
mull --backend=x86-64 -L ./lib -lcustom \\
  --extern=Square=custom_square:1 -p -e 'return Square(7);'
cmake -S mudsl/mull -B build/mull -DMULL_ENABLE_MULIB=OFF
cmake --build build/mull --parallel
cmake --install build/mull --prefix "$HOME/.local"
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
Zero indicates success, one a compiler/runtime/link failure, two invalid
command usage, and 127 a missing worker, configuration, or tool.
Invoked tool/program nonzero statuses are propagated; termination by a signal
is reported as 128 plus the signal number.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR mull (5),
.BR cc (1),
.BR ld (1),
.BR cmake (1)
